# Cryptography: Keeping Secrets in Public

> Cryptography lets two people who have never met exchange secrets
> over a wire tapped by strangers. This lesson covers symmetric and
> asymmetric ciphers, hashing, signatures, and the TLS handshake.

## 1. Plaintext, Ciphertext, and Kerckhoffs's Principle

**Encryption** transforms readable **plaintext** into scrambled
**ciphertext** using an algorithm and a **key**; **decryption** runs
the reverse. The algorithm is public knowledge — the secrecy lives
entirely in the key.

[definition]
Kerckhoffs's principle: a cryptosystem should remain secure even if
everything about it, except the key, is public. Security by secrecy
of the algorithm fails the moment the code leaks; security by key
secrecy survives publication.
[/definition]

[warning]
Never invent your own cipher. Amateur "secret algorithms" are broken
in minutes; modern schemes like AES have survived decades of public
attack precisely because they are published and scrutinised.
[/warning]

## 2. Symmetric Encryption: One Shared Key

In symmetric cryptography both sides hold the **same secret key**:
one key encrypts and decrypts. The workhorse standard is **AES** (the
Advanced Encryption Standard), defined for key sizes of 128, 192, and
256 bits, and fast enough to encrypt disk and network traffic in real
time. Its predecessor **DES** (56-bit key) and triple-DES are
retired — brute force caught up with them.

| Property | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| Keys | one shared secret key |
| Speed | very fast; hardware-accelerated |
| Best at | bulk data — files, disks, session traffic |
| Hard problem | how do two strangers share the key secretly? |
| Standards | AES (128/192/256-bit), ChaCha20 |

The shared-key problem is the reason asymmetric cryptography exists:
you cannot whisper the key across the internet without someone
listening.

## 3. Asymmetric Encryption: Public and Private Keys

Asymmetric (public-key) cryptography uses a **key pair**: a public
key you can post on a billboard and a private key you never share.
Whatever one key locks, only its partner unlocks.

@diagram tree id="crypto-family-tree" layout="tree" aria="Modern cryptography families from symmetric ciphers to hashing and signatures"
  "Modern cryptography" -> "Symmetric — AES, ChaCha20"
  "Modern cryptography" -> "Asymmetric — RSA, ECC, Diffie-Hellman"
  "Modern cryptography" -> "Hashing — SHA-256, SHA-3"
  "Asymmetric — RSA, ECC, Diffie-Hellman" -> "Digital signatures"
  "Asymmetric — RSA, ECC, Diffie-Hellman" -> "Certificates and TLS"
@/diagram

| Property | Symmetric (AES) | Asymmetric (RSA, ECC) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Keys | one shared secret | public and private pair |
| Relative speed | fast | hundreds of times slower |
| Solves | bulk confidentiality | key exchange, signatures |
| Key size for similar strength | 128 bits | RSA 3072 bits, ECC 256 bits |

**RSA** draws its strength from the difficulty of factoring the
product of two huge primes; **ECC** (elliptic-curve cryptography)
achieves similar strength with far shorter keys. Both are too slow
for bulk data, so real systems use them only for the essentials.

## 4. Key Exchange: Strangers Agreeing in the Open

The **Diffie–Hellman key exchange** solves the shared-key problem.
Each party publishes a public value, keeps a secret value, and both
compute the same shared number — yet an eavesdropper who sees every
public message still cannot compute it. The modern variant uses
elliptic curves (ECDHE) for speed and forward secrecy.

Real protocols are **hybrid**: asymmetric cryptography negotiates a
fresh *session key*, then fast symmetric AES encrypts the actual
conversation. That is exactly what TLS does on every HTTPS page.

[tip]
Exam shorthand: asymmetric for the handshake, symmetric for the
conversation. If a question mixes "RSA" with "bulk data", the answer
is hybrid encryption, not RSA alone.
[/tip]

## 5. Hashing Is Not Encryption

A **cryptographic hash function** (SHA-256, SHA-3) digests any input
into a fixed-length fingerprint. Hashing is one-way: you cannot
decrypt a hash, and any change to the input produces a completely
different digest.

| Property | Encryption | Hashing |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Reversible? | yes, with the key | no — one-way |
| Output size | roughly input size | fixed length |
| Purpose | confidentiality | integrity, fingerprints, password storage |
| Examples | AES, ChaCha20, RSA | SHA-256, SHA-3 |

[important]
Systems never store your password — they store its hash with a random
**salt**. The salt defeats precomputed rainbow tables, and slow
purpose-built hashes (bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2) make offline guessing
expensive. Plain fast hashes like MD5 or SHA-1 are considered broken
for this purpose, mainly because collision attacks on them are now
practical.
[/important]

## 6. Digital Signatures

A digital signature inverts everyday encryption: the signer encrypts
a document's hash with their **private** key, and anyone verifies it
with the signer's **public** key. Verification answers three
questions at once — did the document change (integrity), is it really
from the claimed sender (authentication), and can the sender deny it
later (non-repudiation)?

[example]
A software vendor signs an installer. Your OS downloads the file,
verifies the signature against the vendor's public key, and warns you
if even one byte was altered in transit or by an attacker.
[/example]

Note the direction carefully: to *sign*, use your own private key; to
*send secretly to someone*, use their public key. Swapping these is a
classic exam trap.

## 7. Certificates and the HTTPS Handshake

A **digital certificate** is a signed document binding a domain name
to a public key, issued by a **Certificate Authority (CA)** that
browsers already trust. The browser checks the chain: root CA, then
intermediate, then the site certificate — plus validity dates and the
hostname match.

@diagram flowchart id="tls-handshake" layout="hierarchical" aria="Simplified TLS handshake from ClientHello to encrypted session"
node hello label="ClientHello: supported ciphers, random values" shape="pill"
node cert label="ServerHello and certificate" shape="round-rect"
node verify label="Chain valid and hostname matches?" shape="diamond"
node alert label="Browser shows security warning" shape="round-rect"
node exchange label="ECDHE key agreement produces session key" shape="round-rect"
node secure label="AES-encrypted application data flows" shape="pill"

hello -> cert
cert -> verify
verify -> alert label="no"
verify -> exchange label="yes"
exchange -> secure
@/diagram

The padlock therefore means two things: traffic is encrypted, and the
server's identity was vouched for by a chain ending at a trusted root
CA. A self-signed or mismatched certificate breaks the chain — which
is why browsers shout.

## 8. Strength, Key Sizes, and the Future

Strength is about how long brute force would take. Each extra bit
doubles the search space, so symmetric keys grow one bit at a time
while RSA keys must grow far faster to stay equal.

@chart bar title="Comparable key lengths for similar security (bits)" labels:
Symmetric AES
Elliptic curve ECC
RSA

data:
128
256
3072
@/chart

On the horizon, **quantum computers** threaten RSA and ECC by
factoring and solving discrete logarithms quickly; cryptographers are
standardising **post-quantum** algorithms whose hardness survives
quantum attack. Symmetric AES-256 and hash functions are considered
far more resilient — just needing longer output sizes.

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | Remember this |
| --- | --- |
| Kerckhoffs's principle | secrecy lives in the key, not the algorithm |
| AES | symmetric, 128/192/256-bit, fast bulk encryption |
| RSA and ECC | asymmetric; factoring and elliptic-curve hardness |
| Hybrid TLS | asymmetric handshake, symmetric session |
| Diffie–Hellman | strangers derive a shared secret in the open |
| Hash vs encryption | one-way fingerprint vs reversible with key |
| Salt plus slow hash | how passwords should be stored |
| Digital signature | private key signs, public key verifies |
| Certificate | CA-signed binding of domain to public key |
| Quantum threat | endangers RSA/ECC, not AES-256 |
